    	Hazelcast is especially useful when you want a distributed in-memory data grid tightly integrated with Java applications, with distributed collections and
    	computation capabilities.

    	57.	When should caching not be used?
            Caching should not be used when the data changes frequently, when data consistency is critical, or when the cost of maintaining the cache is higher than the
             performance benefit. In such cases, returning stale data can lead to incorrect application behavior.

    	58.	How does Spring decide cache key?
            Spring generates a cache key based on the method parameters. By default, if the method has a single parameter, that parameter is used as the cache key.
             If there are multiple parameters, Spring generates a composite key (SimpleKey) using all the parameters. We can also define a custom cache key using the key
              attribute with a Spring Expression Language (SpEL) expression.

    	59.	Can we customize cache keys?
            Yes, we can customize cache keys using the key attribute of caching annotations such as @Cacheable, @CachePut, and @CacheEvict. The key attribute uses Spring
             Expression Language (SpEL) to define how the cache key should be generated.
    		| Requirement              | Approach              |
            | ------------------------ | --------------------- |
            | Simple custom key        | `key = "#userId"`     |
            | Multiple parameters      | SpEL expression       |
            | Complex/common key logic | Custom `KeyGenerator` |

    	60.	What happens if cache is not found?
           If the requested data is not found in the cache, it is called a cache miss. Spring executes the target method, retrieves the data (typically from the database),
            stores the result in the cache, and then returns it to the client. Subsequent requests with the same key are served directly from the cache.

