103. Explain the concept of auto-configuration in Spring Boot.

     when the Spring Boot application starts, @EnableAutoConfiguration triggers Spring Boot's auto-configuration mechanism. Spring Boot scans the classpath to identify available libraries (such as Spring Web, Spring Data JPA, Hibernate, etc.). It also reads the application.properties or application.yml configuration.

     Based on the available dependencies, configuration properties, and existing beans in the application context, Spring Boot loads the appropriate auto-configuration classes. These classes use conditional annotations such as:

	•	@ConditionalOnClass

	•	@ConditionalOnMissingBean

	•	@ConditionalOnProperty

	•	@ConditionalOnBean

    These conditions determine whether a bean should be created. If the conditions are satisfied, Spring Boot automatically creates and registers the required beans in the Spring container. If we have already defined our own bean, @ConditionalOnMissingBean prevents Spring Boot from creating a duplicate bean.

